window.docContents[246].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Uniqueness-of-unit/#Chapter2___Proposition2___2___3":{"contents":"Proposition 2.2.3. If a unit exists, it is unique.\n\nProof. Let 1, 1' be two units. Then 1 = 11' = 1'. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Uniqueness of unit","header":"Uniqueness of unit","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Uniqueness-of-unit/#Chapter2___Proposition2___2___3"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-quiver/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___4":{"contents":"Definition 2.8.4. The path algebra P_Q of a quiver Q is the algebra whose basis is formed by oriented paths in Q, including the trivial paths p_i, i \\in I, corresponding to the vertices of Q, and multiplication is the concatenation of paths: ab is the path obtained by first tracing b and then a. If two paths cannot be concatenated, the product is defined to be zero.An oriented path is specified by a nonnegative integer n, a sequence of vertices i_0, \\ldots, i_n, and a sequence of edges e_1, \\ldots, e_n such that each e_r has source i_{r-1} and target i_r. In particular, when n = 0, one still must choose one vertex i_0, which explains why there is one p_i for each i \\in I. Two paths a, b can be concatenated to form the path ab if and only if the final target of a equals the first source of b.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Path algebra of a quiver","header":"Path algebra of a quiver","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-quiver/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___4"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Introduction-to-irreducible-representations-of-tensor-products/#Chapter3___Discussion_before_Theorem3___10___2":{"contents":"The following theorem describes irreducible finite dimensional representations of A \\otimes B in terms of irreducible finite dimensional representations of A and those of B.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.10. Representations of tensor products\u0009Introduction to irreducible representations of tensor products","header":"Introduction to irreducible representations of tensor products","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___10___-Representations-of-tensor-products/Introduction-to-irreducible-representations-of-tensor-products/#Chapter3___Discussion_before_Theorem3___10___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_4/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S4":{"contents":"(4) The symmetric group S_4. The order of S_4 is 24, and there are five conjugacy classes: e, (12), (123), (1234), (12)(34). Thus the sum of the squares of the dimensions of five irreducible representations is 24. As with S_3, there are two of dimension 1: the trivial and sign representations, \\mathbb{C}_+ and \\mathbb{C}_-. The other three must then have dimensions 2, 3, and 3. Because S_3 \\cong S_4/\\mathbb{Z}_2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_2, where \\mathbb{Z}_2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_2 is \\{e, (12)(34), (13)(24), (14)(23)\\}, the 2-dimensional representation of S_3 can be pulled back to the 2-dimensional representation of S_4, which we will call \\mathbb{C}^2.\n\nWe can consider S_4 as the group of rotations of a cube acting by permuting the interior diagonals (or, equivalently, on a regular octahedron permuting pairs of opposite faces); this gives the 3-dimensional representation \\mathbb{C}^3_+.\n\nThe last 3-dimensional representation is \\mathbb{C}^3_-, the product of \\mathbb{C}^3_+ with the sign representation. \\mathbb{C}^3_+ and \\mathbb{C}^3_- are different, for if g is a transposition, \\det g|_{\\mathbb{C}^3_+} = 1 while \\det g|_{\\mathbb{C}^3_-} = (-1)^3 = -1. Note\nthat another realization of \\mathbb{C}^3_- is by action of S_4 by symmetries (not necessarily rotations) of the regular tetrahedron. Yet another realization of this representation is the space of functions on the set of four elements (on which S_4 acts by permutations) with zero sum of values.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Irreducible representations of S_4","header":"Irreducible representations of S_4","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-S_4/#Chapter4___Example4___3_S4"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra/#Chapter4___Problem4___5___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.5. Orthogonality of characters\u0009Idempotents from characters in the group algebra\u0009Idempotents from characters in the group algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Idempotents-from-characters-in-the-group-algebra/#Chapter4___Problem4___5___2___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity--Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_--Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The ambient-to-subgroup functor is left adjoint to the subgroup-to-ambient functor.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Introduction_5.10; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.10.1; role=primary]\n\nA linear equivalence between morphisms into a subgroup-to-ambient image and morphisms from an ambient-to-subgroup image.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_Problem5.10.2_parts; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.10.1; role=primary]\n\nUnder the displayed Hom-space equivalence, a morphism is evaluated at a vector by taking its value at the identity element.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.10.1; role=primary]\n\nAn isomorphism between the two displayed module-valued Hom functors on ambient and subgroup representations.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_Problem5.10.2_parts; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Introduction_5.10; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Theorem5.10.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.10. Frobenius reciprocity\u0009Frobenius reciprocity: Hom_G(V, Ind W) ≅ Hom_H(Res V, W)\u0009Frobenius reciprocity: Hom_G(V, Ind W) ≅ Hom_H(Res V, W)\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity/Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___10___-Frobenius-reciprocity--Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_--Frobenius-reciprocity___-Hom_G_LPAR_V___-Ind-W_RPAR_-___-Hom_H_LPAR_Res-V___-W_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions--Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The lexicographic non-strict order on partitions of the same natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_lexicographic_ordering; role=supporting]\n\nThe strict lexicographic order on partitions of the same natural number.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_lexicographic_ordering; role=supporting]\n\nThere exist two partitions such that one is strictly smaller than the other in lexicographic order.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_lexicographic_ordering; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009Definition of lexicographic ordering on partitions\u0009Definition of lexicographic ordering on partitions\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions--Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Complete-reducibility-and-Peter-Weyl-theorem-for-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-_LPAR_continues-to-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Theorem5___23___2":{"contents":"Theorem 5.23.2. (i) Every finite dimensional algebraic representation of GL(V) is completely reducible, and decomposes into summands of the form L_\\lambda (which are pairwise nonisomorphic).\n\n(ii) (The Peter-Weyl theorem for GL(V)) Let R be the algebra of polynomial functions on GL(V). Then as a representation of GL(V) \\times GL(V) (with action (\\rho(g, h)\\phi)(x) = \\phi(g^{-1}xh), g, h, x \\in GL(V), \\phi \\in R), R decomposes as\n\nR = \\bigoplus_\\lambda L_\\lambda^* \\otimes L_\\lambda,\n\nwhere the summation runs over all \\lambda.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\u0009Complete reducibility and Peter-Weyl theorem for GL(V) (continues to missing page)","header":"Complete reducibility and Peter-Weyl theorem for GL(V) (continues to missing page)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Complete-reducibility-and-Peter-Weyl-theorem-for-GL_LPAR_V_RPAR_-_LPAR_continues-to-missing-page_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Theorem5___23___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____--5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations--Section-5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations-heading":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u00095.25.2. 1-dimensional representations","header":"Section 5.25.2: 1-dimensional representations heading","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____--5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations--Section-5___25___2___-1-dimensional-representations-heading"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_--Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The dimension of an induced representation is the subgroup index times the original dimension.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.8.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Dimension of induced representation equals dim V times index (G:H)\u0009Dimension of induced representation equals dim V times index (G:H)\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_--Dimension-of-induced-representation-equals-dim-V-times-index-_LPAR_G___H_RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Cartan-matrix/#Chapter9___Definition9___3___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Cartan matrix\u0009Cartan matrix","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Cartan-matrix/#Chapter9___Definition9___3___1___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Proof-of-Theorem-9___6___4-via-quasi-inverse-functors/#Chapter9___Problem9___6___5":{"contents":"Problem 9.6.5. Let G : B\\text{-fmod} \\to \\mathcal{C} be the functor defined by G(X) := P \\otimes_B X, where P \\otimes_B X is the cokernel of the morphism \\psi : P \\otimes B \\otimes X \\to P \\otimes X given by \\psi = a_P \\otimes \\operatorname{Id} - \\operatorname{Id} \\otimes a_X (where a_P : P \\otimes B \\to P, a_X : B \\otimes X \\to X are the morphisms representing the actions of B on P and X).\n\n(i) Show that F \\circ G \\cong \\operatorname{Id}. That is, for every X \\in B\\text{-fmod}, show that X is naturally isomorphic to \\operatorname{Hom}(P, P \\otimes_B X). (For this you should only need that P is a nonzero projective object.)\n\n(ii) For any X \\in \\mathcal{C}, construct a natural morphism\n\n\\xi : P \\otimes_B \\operatorname{Hom}(P, X) \\to X,\n\nand show that it is surjective.\n\n(iii) Show that G \\circ F \\cong \\operatorname{Id}. To this end, consider the short exact sequence\n\n0 \\to K \\to P \\otimes_B \\operatorname{Hom}(P, X) \\to X \\to 0,\n\nwhere the third map is \\xi. Apply the functor F to this sequence and use (i) to conclude that K = 0 and hence \\xi is an isomorphism. Conclude that the functors G and F are quasi-inverse to each other and hence are equivalences of categories.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Proof of Theorem 9.6.4 via quasi-inverse functors","header":"Proof of Theorem 9.6.4 via quasi-inverse functors","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Proof-of-Theorem-9___6___4-via-quasi-inverse-functors/#Chapter9___Problem9___6___5"},"/Backmatter/Mathematical-references/#Backmatter___MathematicalReferences":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u0009Backmatter\u0009Mathematical references","header":"Mathematical references","id":"/Backmatter/Mathematical-references/#Backmatter___MathematicalReferences"}});